    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The appeal of a cobblestone driveway is easy to understand. It recalls old European streets and courtyards, and natural stone setts, the squared blocks most people mean when they say "cobbles", can be extremely hard-wearing. In the UK, granite setts are a familiar sight on older streets and period homes; in the US the same kind of squared stone is often sold as "Belgian block". It gives a front garden a texture and sense of permanence that tarmac or plain concrete cannot match.</p>
<p>That character comes at a price: cobblestone is usually one of the most expensive driveway surfaces you can choose. What it actually costs varies so much with your region, the supplier, the stone and the site that any figure would quickly date, so this guide explains what makes one cobblestone driveway cost more than another. With that in mind, you can plan sensibly, read quotes with confidence and decide where it is worth spending.</p>

<h2 id="stone">1. The stone you choose</h2>
<p>The type of stone is the main driver of material cost. Genuine cobbles are natural stone, but there are cobble-effect alternatives too, and they sit at very different points on the scale.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Granite setts:</strong> the classic choice for a cobbled driveway. Granite is very hard and copes well with traffic and weather, which is why it is the usual benchmark that other options are compared against.</li>
<li><strong>Basalt and sandstone setts:</strong> basalt gives deep, dark greys and blacks; sandstone gives warmer buffs and browns. Prices are broadly comparable with granite but depend on availability. Not every sandstone is suitable for vehicles, so ask the supplier to confirm the stone and thickness are rated for driveway use.</li>
<li><strong>Reclaimed setts:</strong> salvaged from old streets and yards, they bring genuine age and character. Because they have to be sourced, cleaned of old mortar and tar, and sorted for size and thickness, they often cost more than new stone, and supply can be patchy for a large driveway.</li>
<li><strong>Concrete cobble-effect blocks:</strong> manufactured blocks shaped and coloured to look like setts. They are usually the cheapest option, quicker to lay because they are uniform, and widely available, though they do not have the depth of colour and texture of natural stone, and the finish can wear over time.</li>
</ul>
<p>The finish matters too. Sawn setts with flat tops are usually dearer than cropped (split) setts, and tumbled setts with softened edges sit somewhere between, depending on the supplier. Thicker setts for heavier vehicles cost more than thinner ones.</p>

<!--figure:rounded-cobblestones-closeup|Natural rounded cobbles cost more than sawn setts or concrete cobble-effect blocks.-->

<h2 id="labour">2. Labour and hand-laying</h2>
<p>Labour is usually the largest part of a cobblestone driveway's cost. Unlike tarmac, which is spread and rolled, or concrete, which is poured and levelled, setts are laid by hand. Each stone is selected, placed and tapped down to line and level by a skilled layer.</p>
<p>Natural setts vary in size and shape, so the layer has to keep sorting and matching stones to keep the joints even. It is slow work, and a good-sized driveway can take a crew a number of weeks. Experience shows in the finished surface, so the most experienced installers are rarely the cheapest. Uniform concrete blocks lay noticeably faster than natural stone, which is a large part of why they cost less overall.</p>

<h2 id="preparation">3. Groundwork and sub-base</h2>
<p>A cobblestone driveway is only as good as what lies underneath it. If the foundation is poorly prepared, the stones will rock, sink or lift, and the only fix is lifting and relaying them. Groundwork typically covers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Excavation:</strong> digging out turf, topsoil and any old surface down to firm ground, and taking the spoil away.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-base:</strong> a layer of compacted crushed stone that spreads the load of vehicles and lets water drain. Its depth is not one-size-fits-all; your installer or an engineer will set it for your soil, your climate and the vehicles that will use the drive. Soft ground, clay and cold winters generally mean a deeper build, which means more digging and more stone.</li>
<li><strong>Bedding layer:</strong> a layer of sharp sand, grit or a mortar bed, depending on the system, that the setts are set into at the right height and fall.</li>
<li><strong>Drainage:</strong> every driveway needs a gentle fall away from the house, which your installer will set. Wet or sloping sites may also need a channel drain, a French drain or a soakaway, which can add noticeably to the cost.</li>
</ul>
<p>Breaking out an old concrete or tarmac drive first adds time and disposal costs. Before any digging, buried services need to be located: in the UK, check utility plans or use a cable avoidance tool; in the US, call 811. Our guide to <a href="/blog/cobblestone-driveway-mistakes">cobblestone driveway mistakes</a> explains why this is the worst place to cut corners.</p>

<h2 id="pattern">4. Pattern complexity</h2>
<p>The more intricate the layout, the longer it takes to lay and the more stone is cut and wasted.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stretcher bond (running bond):</strong> straight rows with staggered joints, like brickwork. It is the simplest and usually the most economical pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Herringbone:</strong> setts laid at angles to each other. It locks together well under traffic but needs more cutting at the edges, which adds labour and waste.</li>
<li><strong>Fan (segmental arc) and circles:</strong> setts laid in sweeping arcs or rings. These are the most striking and the most expensive patterns, as they need a skilled layer and considerably more time to keep the curves true.</li>
</ul>
<p>Borders, inset bands in a contrasting stone and curves in the driveway's outline all add cutting time too.</p>

<!--figure:hexagonal-paving-pattern|Fans, arcs and other patterns take longer to lay than simple straight courses.-->

<h2 id="delivery">5. Distance from the supplier</h2>
<p>Stone is heavy, so delivery is a real part of the cost. Setts quarried or stocked relatively close to you are generally cheaper to get to site than stone hauled a long way or imported. Large orders may arrive on a lorry with a crane offload, and if a lorry cannot get close to the driveway, the stone has to be moved by hand or with smaller machinery, which adds time.</p>
<p>Local labour markets matter as well: installers in areas with higher living costs tend to charge more, and specialist sett layers may travel further for a job, which can be reflected in the quote.</p>

<h2 id="jointing">6. Jointing method</h2>
<p>The material in the gaps between the setts affects the cost, the look and how much upkeep the driveway needs later.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sand or grit joints:</strong> the traditional and usually the cheapest option. They are flexible and let water through, but need topping up from time to time and can let weeds in.</li>
<li><strong>Resin or polymeric jointing compounds:</strong> they set firm to resist weeds and washing out, cost more than sand and need careful application to the manufacturer's instructions. Some are permeable and some are not, so check.</li>
<li><strong>Mortar joints:</strong> a neat, solid finish that suits a rigid (bound) construction designed for it. It is the most labour-intensive option, and on the wrong base it tends to crack. Mortar-jointed setts are not permeable.</li>
</ul>

<!--figure:wet-stone-setts-joints|Joints can be sand-filled or mortared; mortar costs more but holds up to heavy traffic.-->

<h2 id="edging">7. Edge haunching and kerbs</h2>
<p>A cobbled driveway needs a firm edge to stop the setts creeping outwards under traffic. That usually means an edge course of setts or kerbs bedded on concrete, with a concrete haunch along the outside. It is easy to forget when budgeting, but a long or curving edge adds materials and skilled time, and it is not something to drop to save money.</p>

<h2 id="access">8. Access, waste and permissions</h2>
<p>Site access affects almost every line of the quote. A narrow entrance, steps or no room for a skip means more barrowing and more time. Excavated soil, an old surface and offcuts all have to be removed, so skips or grab lorries are usually priced separately.</p>
<p>Permissions can add to the cost and the timetable. In England, a new or replacement hard surface over 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area, such as a lawn or soakaway, within the property. Sand- or grit-jointed setts on a free-draining base can be designed to qualify; mortar joints usually will not. A new dropped kerb needs your council's permission, and the council or its approved contractor normally carries out the work on the pavement. In the US, check local permits and any HOA rules. Wherever you are, check with your local authority before work starts.</p>

<h2 id="comparing-quotes">9. How to get quotes you can compare</h2>
<p>Quotes are only comparable if they price the same job. Give two or three installers the same measurements and specification, and ask each for an itemised quote covering:</p>
<ul>
<li>The area, and the depth and material of each layer: sub-base, bedding and any drainage layer.</li>
<li>The setts: stone type, finish, size, thickness, colour and source, plus an allowance for cuts and waste.</li>
<li>The laying pattern, and any borders, bands or curves.</li>
<li>Excavation, breaking out any old surface, and skip or spoil removal.</li>
<li>Drainage: the fall, plus any channel drains, French drains or soakaways.</li>
<li>Jointing material and method.</li>
<li>Edge courses, kerbs and haunching.</li>
<li>Delivery, offloading and how the stone gets from the lorry to the driveway.</li>
<li>Planning or dropped kerb applications, and who makes them.</li>
<li>Making good of lawns, borders and paths afterwards, and any workmanship guarantee.</li>
</ul>
<p>If one quote is much lower than the others, check which of these items it leaves out or scales back, especially the depth of the sub-base, the sett thickness and the edge restraint.</p>

<h2 id="keeping-costs-down">10. Ways to keep the cost down</h2>
<ul>
<li>Choose a simple stretcher bond and keep the fan or circle pattern for a small feature area, such as a turning circle or the entrance.</li>
<li>Use natural setts only where they are seen most, such as a border, the apron at the entrance or a band across the drive, with concrete cobble-effect blocks or a gravel surface elsewhere.</li>
<li>Ask your installer about stone that is stocked or quarried closer to you, in a similar colour to the one you like.</li>
<li>Keep the driveway's outline simple; curves and extra bays add edging and cutting.</li>
<li>Reuse sound existing kerbs or edging where your installer confirms they are in good condition.</li>
<li>Do the light jobs yourself, such as clearing plants or making good the borders afterwards, and leave the excavation, base and laying to a professional.</li>
<li>Do not save on the sub-base, drainage or edge restraint. They cost far less to get right first time than to fix later.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask each installer to price the setts as a separate line, with a second line for a cobble-effect concrete block in the same pattern. You can then see exactly what natural stone adds to your quote before you decide.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="long-term-value">11. Long-term value vs upfront cost</h2>
<p>Against tarmac or plain concrete, the upfront difference is large. The fairer comparison is over the life of the driveway. Well-laid natural setts on a sound base need relatively little day-to-day care: occasional weeding, topping up sand joints and a gentle clean. A sealed surface may need resealing or resurfacing over time, while an individual damaged sett can usually be lifted and replaced without disturbing the rest. How long any driveway lasts depends on the installation, the use it gets, your climate and how well it is maintained.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does a cobblestone driveway add value to my home?</h3>
<p>A well-built cobblestone driveway can add kerb appeal and make a good first impression on buyers, particularly on period homes. Whether it adds more to the value than it costs depends on your property, your local market and the condition of the drive when you sell, so it is best chosen because you will enjoy it rather than as a guaranteed financial return.</p>
<h3>How long does a cobblestone driveway last?</h3>
<p>That depends on the stone, the quality of the base and laying, the traffic, your climate and maintenance. Dense natural stones such as granite and basalt are very durable, and centuries-old cobbled streets show what well-laid setts can do, but the life of your driveway rests on how well it is built.</p>
<h3>Is it harder to clear snow from cobblestones?</h3>
<p>A little. The textured surface catches a metal shovel blade, so a plastic shovel is kinder to the stone. For de-icers, use products the stone supplier or installer approves, avoid rock salt near metalwork and on any newly laid mortar, and avoid ammonium-based de-icers.</p>
<h3>Can I lay a cobblestone driveway myself?</h3>
<p>A small path or border is a realistic DIY project, but a full driveway is hard work to do well: the base has to carry vehicles, the falls have to drain, and laying natural setts neatly takes skill. For most people, a professional is the safer choice. If you do take on part of the work, cut stone with a wet saw and wear eye and ear protection and a suitable respirator, as stone dust contains silica, and follow the hire company's safety guidance for any machinery.</p>
<h3>Are cobblestone driveways permeable?</h3>
<p>They can be, if the setts are laid with sand or grit joints on a free-draining base rather than on a mortar bed with mortar joints. A permeable design lets rainwater soak into the ground and, in England, can help a front-garden driveway avoid the need for planning permission. Agree the system with your installer at the design stage.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>The cost of a cobblestone driveway comes down to the stone, the hand-laying, the groundwork, the pattern and how easy your site is to work on, with delivery, jointing and edging making up the rest. An itemised quote for the same specification is the clearest way to see where your money goes. Before you commit, read our guide to <a href="/blog/cobblestone-driveway-mistakes">cobblestone driveway mistakes</a>, and if you are still weighing up surfaces, compare the <a href="/blog/paver-driveway-pros-and-cons">pros and cons of a paver driveway</a> or what goes into the <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-cost">cost of a gravel driveway</a>. You can also explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your front garden with a designer.</p>
